  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound` Explained\n\n## What is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an efficient algorithm for finding a target value within a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (which would take O(n) time), binary search repeatedly divides the search interval in half:\n\n1. Look at the middle element.\n2. If it matches the target, you're done.\n3. If the target is smaller, discard the right half.\n4. If the target is larger, discard the left half.\n5. Repeat until the target is found or the interval is empty.\n\nThis gives a **time complexity of O(log n)**, which is dramatically faster than linear search for large lists.\n\n---\n\n## What is `lower_bound`?\n\nThe `lower_bound` function returns the **index of the first element that is greater than or equal to** the target value. If no such element exists (i.e., all elements are smaller than the target), it returns the length of the list \u2014 indicating where the target *should* be inserted to maintain sorted order.\n\nThis is exactly what Python's built-in `bisect_left` does. We'll implement it from scratch.\n\n---\n\n## Implementation",
